course: dictionary
lesson: 424
title: Sliding on a wet road
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Some words describe things you can see, and others describe what happens to things. Aquaplane is a word for something that happens to a car in the rain. «A layer of water between tyres and road makes a car skid; this is called aquaplaning.» The clues are «layer of water» and «between tyres and road», so you can work out the idea before you look anything up.
explain: Good readers ask what a word is doing in the sentence. Here aquaplaning is the name given to the skid caused by that layer of water. Try your idea in the sentence: if the water is what makes the car slide, the meaning fits.
explain: Some words can be more than one part of speech. Aquaplane can be a verb (a car aquaplanes) or a noun (an old board pulled behind a boat). Read the words around it to decide which meaning the writer means.
fact: Aquaplaning happens when a car's tyres slide on a layer of water on a wet road.
fact: When a car aquaplanes, the driver cannot steer or brake well because the tyres have lost grip.
fact: Tyre treads push water away, so «worn-smooth "bald" tyres are dangerous in the rain».
fact: Drivers slow down in heavy rain to avoid aquaplaning.
fact: Old meaning: an aquaplane was a board towed by a motorboat that a person stood on, like early water-skiing. In the US people often say «hydroplane» instead.
exercise: What happens when a car aquaplanes? | *The tyres slide on a layer of water and the driver loses grip | The engine stops because it is too cold | The car turns into a boat | The tyres stick firmly and the car goes faster
exercise: What do tyre treads do in the rain? | Make the car louder | *Push water away from the tyres | Hold the car up in the air | Heat the road
exercise: In «A layer of water between tyres and road makes a car skid; this is called aquaplaning», what is aquaplaning? | *The name for that skid caused by water | A board pulled by a boat | A sunny day at the seaside | A type of petrol station
test: Why are worn-smooth "bald" tyres dangerous in the rain? | They look old | They are too heavy | *They push water away less well, so grip is lost | They make the car louder
test: Which is the old meaning of aquaplane as a noun? | A kind of car | *A board towed by a motorboat that a person stood on | A wet day in Scotland | A layer of water on a road
exam: Mia reads «aquaplane» in a car story and in a boat story. Which meaning fits the car story? | *The tyres slide on water and the driver loses grip | A petrol station | A type of sailing boat | A board towed by a motorboat
